{"result": {"url": "https://openbooks.net/reviews/glassy-eyed", "title": "Review: Glassy-eyed — Brittle Paper", "text": "Review by Doreen Baingana. Brittle Paper, March 2023. Editor's Choice.

'Ama Owusu's debut is built around a simple and quietly devastating premise: a family is the thing that passes through a workshop. Three generations of the Boateng-Asante women move through the same physical space in different decades, and Owusu uses the continuity of the workshop to make visible what changes and what does not.

The glass is everywhere in this novel, and it is never merely symbolic. Owusu is exact about the craft — the temperature of the furnace, the timing of the gather, the way a piece can look finished and then crack at the annealing stage — and this exactness gives the metaphorical weight its purchase.' — Doreen Baingana"}, "actions": [], "error": null}

{"result": {"url": "https://openbooks.net/books/glassy-eyed", "title": "Glassy-eyed — Cassava Press", "text": "Publisher page. Glassy-eyed by Ama Owusu. Cassava Press, 2023. Paperback, 287 pages. ISBN: 978-1-73625-441-8.

Kofi Boateng built his glass-blowing workshop on the outskirts of Kumasi in 1963. Glassy-eyed follows three generations of the Boateng-Asante family through the workshop Kofi built: his daughter Abena, who inherits it during the structural adjustment years of the 1990s and nearly loses it; and Abena's daughter Nana, who returns to it in the present day.

Praise: 'One of the most impressive debuts from West Africa in years.' — The Africa Report. 'Precise, unhurried, and quietly devastating.' — Brittle Paper. Longlisted, Etisalat Prize for Literature, 2023."}, "actions": [], "error": null}
